/*
 * Description: three sections in the order the team set, The Problem, Our Idea,
 * How MOSKATEER Works. The text is the team's, as sent.
 */

const SECTIONS: WikiSection[] = [
  {
    id: "the-problem",
    title: "The Problem",
    band: "mist",
    body: (
      <>
        <P>
          Malaria remains one of the most significant infectious disease burdens worldwide, with an estimated 282 million cases and 610,000 deaths recorded globally in 2024. The case of India is unique in that it illustrates both the progress and the fragility of current control efforts. Recent data suggests that the case incidence has fallen by over 90% since 2000, yet an estimated 2 million cases still occurred in 2024, with both incidence and mortality rising again year-on-year (Severe Malaria Observatory, 2025).
        </P>
        <P>
          Existing vector control strategies like insecticide-treated nets, indoor residual spraying, and larval source management act downstream of the transmission cycle and their efficacy is increasingly compromised by evolving insecticide resistance across Anopheles populations, driven by both target-site mutations and metabolic detoxification mechanisms (Abbasi &amp; Daliri, 2025). This erosion of frontline tools has intensified interest, among many, in genetic approaches that act directly on the vector itself.
        </P>
        <P>
          CRISPR-based gene drives represent one of the most powerful genetic strategies proposed to date, using super-Mendelian inheritance to spread a trait, typically sterility or a lethal phenotype, through a wild population far faster than natural selection. However, this mechanism is what makes gene drives difficult to contain, whenever necessary. Reversal strategies such as e-CHACRs, ERACRs, and anti-Cas9 proteins remain theoretical. (Naidoo, Oliver et al., 2025). Consequently, no self-propagating gene drive has yet been released into a wild Anopheles population. (Connolly et al., 2024).
        </P>
        <P>
          This leaves vector control caught in a structural trade-off. Interventions strong enough to durably suppress transmission at the population level have so far required a genetic change to the wild vector that is self-propagating and effectively irreversible, while interventions that preserve reversibility and containment have, until now, lacked comparable population-level reach.
        </P>
      </>
    ),
  },

  {
    id: "our-idea",
    title: "Our Idea",
    band: "paper",
    body: (
      <>
        <P>
          The trade-off outlined above that population-level efficacy has so far required an irreversible, self-propagating genetic change to the wild vector, defines the question we choose to address: Could a comparable effect be achieved without modifying the mosquito genome at all?
        </P>
        <P>
          This constraint led us to paratransgenesis, engineering a microorganism associated with the mosquito to carry the functional payload. Systematic evaluation of paratransgenesis as a vector control strategy has identified it as a viable complement to existing malaria control approaches. But establishing the fact that the payload lives in a bacterium rather than the mosquito genome only solves half the problem; the payload itself still needs to act on the mosquito without becoming a permanent, heritable change. This is what led us to RNA interference (RNAi) as the functional mechanism. Rather than cutting or rewriting a target gene the way a CRISPR-based edit does, RNAi suppresses the gene at the level of expression, and only for as long as the silencing RNA is actively being produced by the bacterium. Pairing the two decisions together, a bacterial chassis carrying an RNAi payload implies that the entire intervention can remain governed by something outside the mosquito’s own genome: control the bacteria, and you control the effect.
        </P>
        <P>
          That still leaves one question unanswered: how do you get the bacteria into the mosquito in the first place? We answer this with a sugar trap.
        </P>
      </>
    ),
  },

  {
    id: "how-it-works",
    title: "How MOSKATEER Works",
    band: "paper",
    body: (
      <>
        <P>
          MOSKATEER is built around three connected components: a bacterial chassis engineered to produce and package an RNAi payload, a secretion system that packages this payload into vesicles the bacteria release on their own, and a sugar trap that gets the bacteria into the mosquito population.
        </P>
        <P>
          As a proof of concept, we are building and validating the system in E. coli DH5α, with the intention of ultimately replicating the results in Asaia bogorensis, a bacterium that naturally colonizes the Anopheles mosquito midgut and is already established in the paratransgenesis literature as a stable, mosquito-associated chassis.
        </P>
        <P>
          At the center of the system's biological effect is mosGILT, a gene Anopheles mosquitoes require for normal reproduction. Our chassis is engineered to produce double-stranded RNA (dsRNA) targeting mosGILT, which when delivered into mosquito tissue, triggers RNA interference and silences the gene's expression. Because this suppresses gene expression, the effect depends entirely on the continued delivery of dsRNA by the bacteria, which is what keeps the system reversible.
        </P>
        <P>
          Getting that dsRNA out of the bacterium and into the mosquito is handled by an intricately designed fusion protein construct. The construct includes MS2 coat protein (MS2CP), which binds dsRNA directly through a pair of RNA stem-loops native to the MS2 bacteriophage system, giving  a way to specifically capture and hold onto the RNAi payload inside the cell. MS2CP is fused to a membrane-targeting sequence (MTS), a short helical peptide  which anchors the entire MS2CP–dsRNA complex to the bacterial membrane.
        </P>
        <Figure svg={figCargo} wide draw />
        <P>
          In effect, the MTS acts as an address label, and the dsRNA hitches a ride to that address by binding MS2CP.
        </P>
        <P>
          Once localized to the membrane, the construct is positioned to be captured during outer membrane vesicle (OMV) formation, a natural process in which Gram-negative bacteria like E. coli bud off small, membrane-bound vesicles from their surface, packaging select membrane and periplasmic contents inside as they go. Because our fusion construct is anchored at the membrane, it and the dsRNA bound to it  gets swept into these vesicles as they form and are released extracellularly, giving us a natural bacterial export pathway to carry the RNAi payload out of the cell.
        </P>
        <P>
          To increase how much payload actually gets exported this way, we also included HlyF in our construct. HlyF is a known driver of hypervesiculation in E. coli, it has been shown to substantially increase the rate of outer membrane vesicle biogenesis in extraintestinal pathogenic E. coli and including it in our system boosted the yield of dsRNA-loaded vesicles released per bacterium, directly increasing the amount of functional payload reaching the mosquito.
        </P>
        <Figure svg={figVesicles} wide draw />
        <P>
          Realizing this design, however, required resolving a distinct engineering problem, namely, how a molecule produced inside a bacterial cell could be exported, transported across the full physiological extent of a mosquito's body, and delivered specifically to the tissue where it exerts its effect. We detail this delivery pathway next, tracing it from bacterial export through to its final site of action in the mosquito ovary.
        </P>
        <P>
          Delivery into the mosquito population starts at the sugar trap, which uses acetoin and  sulcatone specifically to attract male mosquitoes. Males ingest the engineered bacteria from the trap; from the male’s gut, the bacteria and vesicles enter the hemolymph and travel to the male's reproductive tract. During mating, they transfer into the female mosquito. Once inside the female, they again enter the hemolymph, which distributes them throughout her body, including to the ovaries, where the dsRNA-loaded vesicles deliver their payload and silence mosGILT, disrupting the female's reproductive capacity.
        </P>
        <Figure svg={figSugarTrap} wide draw />
        <P>
          Taken together, this gives MOSKATEER two distinct routes of spread beyond the initial sugar-trap exposure. The male-to-female mating route described above is the horizontal transmission route: a single male mosquito, exposed once at the sugar trap, can transfer the payload venereally to every female he mates with over his lifetime, multiplying the effect of one trap visit across many individuals in the population.
        </P>
        <Figure svg={figSpread} wide draw />
        <P>
          Once a female has been exposed this way, the system also spreads vertically  from that female to her own offspring. Because the engineered bacteria and their OMVs distribute through the female's hemolymph and colonize her tissues broadly, they are positioned to be passed transovarially into her eggs without needing to re-infect each new generation independently. This extends MOSKATEER's reach across generations rather than stopping at the individuals directly exposed.
        </P>
        <Figure svg={figOffspring} wide draw />
      </>
    ),
  },
];

export function Description() {
  return <WikiPage slug="description" titleArt="delivery-construct" title="Description" sections={SECTIONS} />;
}
